  "body": "In IRC @piscisaureus brought up that it's probably optimal for certain system-level streams to have their own implementations of `wait()`, `read()`, and `write()`, which manage the buffer in their own OS-level way. E.g. a kernel-level TCP buffer.\n\nThis is allowed right now, but only implicitly. That is, you can subclass `ReadableStream`, override `wait()` and `read()` to do OS-level stuff, and override the constructor to not use the internal-buffer push and pull. And it will still work with all existing streams, and it will inherit a `pipeTo` method that works well (because `pipeTo` is generic in `this.read` and `this.wait`). And so on. But it is not clear from the spec exactly how this would work or how to do it.\n",
